"── 86_sunit_runner.mst — WG9: discovery, and a headless runner ──────────
 upstream `6536294` S2/S3, ported. Two things `85_sunit.mst` deliberately does
 not have: a way to FIND the test classes, and a way to run them that answers
 rather than prints.

 WHY FINDING MATTERS. `99_run_all.mst` names all sixty-odd test classes by hand,
 which is fine for a file that is edited whenever a test file is added and is
 useless to anything else — a Tests tab cannot read a list that only exists as
 the body of a script. A test class someone writes in the Browser at three in
 the afternoon has to be runnable at three-oh-one, and nothing will have edited
 `99_run_all.mst` in between.

 WHY ANSWERING MATTERS. `TestRunner report` prints to the Transcript and calls
 `Smalltalk quit:`. Both are right for a cargo run and fatal for a GUI: a Tests
 tab that ran the suite would kill the process it was drawing in. So the running
 here is separated from the reporting, and what it answers is a STRING — because
 the result has to cross the worker seam to the UI VM, and that seam carries
 pickled payloads rather than object graphs.

 THE LINE FORMAT is `Name|run|failed|firstFailure`, one class per line. Flat and
 boring on purpose: it is parsed on the far side of a seam by code that cannot
 see these classes, so anything richer would need a schema both halves agree on.
 A `|` in a failure message would break it, which is why the message is the LAST
 field — a split that keeps the remainder intact cannot be confused by one."

Object subclass: TestRunner [

    TestRunner class >> allTestClasses ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        "Every class whose superclass chain reaches TestCase, TestCase itself
         excluded — it is the base, not a suite, and running it would report a
         zero-assertion pass that means nothing.

         By the CHAIN rather than by direct superclass, so a project that puts
         its own `MyProjectTestCase` in between still has its tests found. That
         is not hypothetical: it is the first thing anyone does after writing
         three test classes with the same setup."
        | out |
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        ClassMirror allClasses do: [ :c |
            (c ~~ TestCase and: [ self class: c inheritsFrom: TestCase ])
                ifTrue: [ out add: c ] ].
        ^out
    ]

    TestRunner class >> class: aClass inheritsFrom: anAncestor ^ <Boolean> [
        "Walks up, stopping at nil. PURE over two classes, so the rule is
         checkable without running a single test."
        | c |
        c := aClass.
        [ c isNil ] whileFalse: [
            c == anAncestor ifTrue: [ ^true ].
            c := c superclass ].
        ^false
    ]

    TestRunner class >> namesOfTestClasses ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        ^self allTestClasses collect: [ :c | c name asString ] ]

    TestRunner class >> runOne: aClass ^ <Array> [
        "One class, its own TestResult. `{class name. run. failed. failures}`.

         ITS OWN result, not the shared `Total`: a Tests tab shows a row per
         class, and a shared counter can only say how many assertions ran in
         the whole image. It also means one class can be re-run on its own
         after a fix, which is the loop anyone actually works in."
        | r |
        r := TestResult new.
        r init.
        [ aClass runWith: r ] on: Error do: [ :e |
            "A test that RAISES rather than failing an assertion would abort
             the sweep and take every later class with it. Recorded as a
             failure of this class instead — the same reason the cookbook
             wraps each block, and the same consequence if it did not."
            r fail: aClass name asString , ': raised ' , e messageText ].
        ^Array with: aClass name asString with: r runCount with: r failCount with: r failures
    ]

    TestRunner class >> runAllReporting ^ <String> [
        "Every discovered class, one line each. Answers rather than prints, and
         never quits — see the file note."
        ^self reportFor: self allTestClasses
    ]

    TestRunner class >> reportFor: classes <Collection> ^ <String> [
        | s |
        s := WriteStream on: String new.
        classes do: [ :c | | row |
            row := self runOne: c.
            s nextPutAll: (row at: 1);
              nextPut: $|; nextPutAll: (row at: 2) printString;
              nextPut: $|; nextPutAll: (row at: 3) printString;
              nextPut: $|; nextPutAll: (self firstFailureOf: (row at: 4)).
            s nl ].
        ^s contents
    ]

    TestRunner class >> firstFailureOf: failures <Collection> ^ <String> [
        "The FIRST failure only, and one line of it. A class with forty broken
         assertions has one broken idea in it nine times out of ten, and forty
         lines in a pane is how the tenth case gets missed too.

         Newlines and pipes are flattened rather than escaped: this crosses a
         line-oriented seam, and a failure message that split its own row would
         be a parse bug reported as a test result."
        | raw s |
        failures isEmpty ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        raw := failures first.
        s := WriteStream on: String new.
        raw do: [ :ch |
            (ch = Character nl or: [ ch asInteger = 13 or: [ ch = $| ] ])
                ifTrue: [ s nextPut: $  ]
                ifFalse: [ s nextPut: ch ] ].
        ^s contents
    ]

    TestRunner class >> runNamed: aName <String> ^ <String> [
        "One class by NAME, for the Tests tab's re-run-this-one. Answers the
         same line format so both paths parse identically — a second format for
         the single-class case is a second parser and a second thing to drift."
        | hit |
        hit := nil.
        self allTestClasses do: [ :c | c name asString = aName ifTrue: [ hit := c ] ].
        hit isNil ifTrue: [ ^aName , '|0|1|no such test class in this image' , (String with: Character nl) ].
        ^self reportFor: (Array with: hit)
    ]
]
